[Music]  hi everyone  thanks for joining me i'm really excited  to be here i'm actually chatting with  you  from my apartment in cambridge  massachusetts  so it's a beautiful day here and i just  wanted to talk to you  a little bit about smartphones  and public health and the digital  frontier  and part of what i'm going to talk to  you about today is actually drawing from  my new book i'm a sociologist a writer  and an editor  and i recently uh published this book  that i'm showing you here on my screen  called the smartphone society technology  power and resistance in the new gilded  age  so thinking about our smartphones  actually this is an apt conversation in  part because  you might even be watching this talk on  your smartphone  but if we think about this very strange  moment  that we're in uh where millions perhaps  billions of us have been  sent indoors by this terrible pandemic  i think it's really highlighted for many  people  perhaps in a way that they weren't  quite aware of before what a deep  relationship they have  with their digital devices particularly  smartphones  and perhaps your relationship to your  smartphone  um has become even stronger  in the past couple months i think that  if we think about this relationship with  our digital devices  we might have mixed feelings so for  example  i think many of us could quickly come up  with some positive  dynamics that are occurring as a result  of our relationship with our smartphones  in the most basic sense  our smartphones are providing a really  crucial  source of information about this  horrible pandemic  but also just what types of strategies  our communities and governments are  implementing  to help quell the virus it's also  providing a really important lifeline to  loved ones and this is particularly  important for people who are struggling  with mental illness as a result  of social isolation or illnesses  that are being aggravated by social  isolation particularly the ld and  elderly  and those struggling with mental health  uh health  issues so we can think about that  and also just for the rest of us  particularly younger people  who are living alone our smartphones and  our digital connections are providing  much needed entertainment and  interaction  but at the same time we can also um  certainly think about some of the maybe  not so good things that are coming  uh with our increased reliance on our  digital  devices in particular if we think about  our  connection with our devices in terms of  information retrieval  certainly those who have a bit of a hard  time putting down their smartphones  might find themselves  more attached than ever to their digital  devices as we  helplessly scroll through the news feed  looking at the case count of our city  and our state and our country  um and really uh kind of falling into  these  news holes where we're just reading uh  you know  we already had this kind of 24-hour news  cycle but we  might find ourselves being sucked in  even worse uh than before  which uh might aggravate mental illness  uh force for some so if we think about  uh it could be good for some and and not  so good for  for others in terms of our addiction to  our smartphones  and we might also uh not necessarily  be retrieving accurate information or  getting a clear sense of  of what's actually happening around us  from the the types of  news articles and stories that we hear  you might have read recently  that approximately half of the  sort of traffic and accounts tweeting  about the coronavirus are likely  to be generated by bots so you're seeing  these threads and conversations  happening  on twitter about the coronavirus but  researchers at carnegie melligan  mellon suggest that about half of these  are are not  conversations that are coming from real  people  but today i'd like to talk to you about  a slightly different  way of of thinking about our devices  and also a sort of broader topic of  concern  which is this question of public health  and smartphones and in particular the  very rapid uptake  and implementation this is happening in  real time  of country by countries and companies  often working in sort of public private  partnerships around the world to  actually use our smartphones  as a way to quell the coronavirus  so i think that this is a pressing issue  not least because  it really highlights some of the  tensions between  privacy and public health and also  the tensions inherent in our  relationship with our digital devices  and the types of surveillance that could  be implemented  at this time you know in the name  of keeping us safe but  in a way that's not entirely clear and i  think that this is  one of the overwhelming kind of  dynamics that me me personally as i try  to get a clear picture  of what governments and companies around  the world  are doing in terms of using our  smartphones  and the data that we generate with them  in order to  create apps or enforce quarantines  and and implement a variety of measures  to  try to quell the virus outbreak  it's not uh it's not entirely clear  what actually is being done how our data  is being used  and and your average person  even you know community groups um  and and state governments are not really  a part of this conversation or allowing  for an  open conversation about what measures  we think are should be implemented and  what measures we're not so sure about  so if we think about some of the  strategies that  governments are using i put this slide  up here  dave gershkorn over at 1-0 made this  nice map  where he just said just a snapshot right  we say at least 30 countries are ramping  up  surveillance to combat the corona virus  and we see a wide variety of strategies  being implemented around the world so  one of the ways that our smartphones  are being used um is in  quarantine enforcement as i mentioned uh  just a minute ago  so if we think about a place like taiwan  they implemented an electronic fence  model  